<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-05">
    <title>Week 5</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-chap4-2ndhalf">
        <title>Chapter 4</title>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 7 Covers 4.7 and 4.8</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <q>Variable Mass</q> problems can be quite tricky to solve. One main
                        challenge is to remember that you need to be clear about your system
                        <em>before</em> applying Newton's 2nd law/Conservation of momentum to it.
                        The other tricky part is that the specific system (meaning: the specific
                        set of particles) that you care about can change with time.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        K&amp;K provide a good algorithm here, and it's worth following it. Determine
                        the relevant system at time <m>t</m>. If there is mass flow into or out of the system,
                        you should include the mass that will flow in or out in your definition of system. Then,
                        <em>keeping the system fixed</em>, draw a pair of pictures representing the impulse-momentum
                        relationship over a small time <m>dt</m>, and see what you learn from that.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 4.8 then follows with a very cool application: the motion of a rocket. The logarithm
                        that comes from the integration also shows why space travel is hard. The fuel mass required
                        grows exponentially with the change in speed.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 4.9 and 4.10 are optional: the examples are very cool and the brief introduction
                        to thermodynamics is exciting! Time permitting, we'll discuss some of this in class.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
</section>